---
# Daily backup of every Longhorn volume to B2.
#
{# DECIDED: groups: [default], and no recurringJobSelector on the StorageClass; Longhorn already
   assigns unlabelled volumes to default, and StorageClass parameters are immutable. #}
# groups: [default] rather than a bespoke group label. Longhorn applies a `default`-group job
# to any volume that has no job of its own, so a new PVC is backed up without anyone
# remembering to label it — and the labels Longhorn reads live on its Volume CRs, not on the
# PVC, so a PVC-level label would have been silently inert.
#
# The trade is that an opt-out is now the explicit act: slice 4's media volumes are large and
# already covered by Kopia on host paths, so they get their own group to keep them out of this
# one. Design doc §6 gate: a service's data must be visible in its NEW backup path before the
# Docker copy is decommissioned.
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: RecurringJob
metadata:
  name: daily-backup
  namespace: longhorn-system
spec:
  # Required, and separate from metadata.name — Longhorn uses spec.name to label the volumes
  # a job claims. Omitting it is rejected with "invalid job {Name: ...}", where the empty Name
  # is the whole complaint.
  name: daily-backup
  cron: "{{ k3s_longhorn_backup_cron }}"
  task: backup
  groups:
    - default
  retain: {{ k3s_longhorn_backup_retain }}
  concurrency: 1
---
# The weekly tier, sharded across weekdays (sixth cap event, 2026-08-16): one Sunday batch of
# 11 volumes — stacked with the daily tier, double retention pruning, and the 07:30 reboot
# resync — blew both B2 2,500/day caps. Each B2-destined volume backs up once a week, on the
# weekday its entry's weekly_backup_claims declares (k3s_longhorn_weekly_volumes); the label
# reconcile job (the `longhorn-reconcile` role) assigns the matching weekly-backup-d<N> group. An hour after the daily
# run so the two never overlap.
{% for shard in range(7) %}
{% if not loop.first %}
---
{% endif %}
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: RecurringJob
metadata:
  name: {{ shard | weekly_backup_group }}
  namespace: longhorn-system
spec:
  name: {{ shard | weekly_backup_group }}
  cron: "{{ k3s_longhorn_weekly_backup_minute_hour }} * * {{ shard }}"
  task: backup
  groups:
    - {{ shard | weekly_backup_group }}
  retain: {{ k3s_longhorn_weekly_backup_retain }}
  concurrency: 1
{% endfor %}
---
# Longhorn's own filesystem trim (H2.2a). It hands blocks that ext4 has already freed back to the
# replicas. The host trim cron in `setup/k3s` keeps running beside it until H2.2c, and a second
# trim of the same day is harmless.
#
# A volume joins this job through the per-job label `recurring-job.longhorn.io/trim: enabled`,
# which `longhorn-reconcile` sets on a volume that is attached and has a Running ReplicaSet or
# StatefulSet workload. Until H2.2b the reconcile job bounds that to one canary claim
# (`longhorn_reconcile_trim_claims`).
{# DECIDED: groups: [] and never a `trim` group. Four readers treat every
   `recurring-job-group.longhorn.io/` key on a Volume as a backup tier, so a `trim` group would put
   a `no-backup` volume back into the restore drill's IN_BACKUP_GROUP set and would replace
   `default` or the weekly shard as the reapers' owner, because `trim` sorts after both.
   longhorn-manager v1.12.1 also runs a job for each volume labelled
   `recurring-job.longhorn.io/<job>=enabled`, and no reader reads that per-job key. Full
   analysis: the H2 plan, *Native trim*. #}
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: RecurringJob
metadata:
  name: trim
  namespace: longhorn-system
spec:
  name: trim
  # 06:10 UTC follows both backup windows (03:30 daily, 04:30 weekly): a trim before the day's
  # snapshot hands back blocks that the snapshot then pins again.
  cron: "{{ k3s_longhorn_trim_cron }}"
  task: filesystem-trim
  groups: []
  # A trim keeps nothing, so the count is unused; the field is written for explicitness.
  retain: 0
  concurrency: 1
